1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
Hi, Rob. How's the course going?

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:07,400
Oh, hi, Mia. Yeah, great. I can't believe the first term's nearly over.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:13,350
I saw your group's performance last night at the student theatre. It was good.

4
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:19,078
Really? Yeah... but now we have to write a report on the whole thing, an in-depth analysis.

5
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:21,100
I don't know where to start.

6
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,620
Like I have to write about the role I played

7
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,110
the doctor, how I developed the character.

8
00:00:26,110 --> 00:00:28,540
Well, what was your starting point?

9
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:34,500
Er... my grandfather was a doctor before he retired, and I just based it on him.

10
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,220
OK, but how? Did you talk to him about it?

11
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:44,269
He must have all sorts of stories, but he never says much about his work, even now.

12
00:00:44,269 --> 00:00:46,760
He has a sort of authority though.

13
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,780
So how did you manage to capture that?

14
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:55,020
I'd... I'd visualise what he must have been like in the past,

15
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:59,260
when he was sitting in his consulting room listening to his patients.

16
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:03,150
OK, so that's what you explain in your report.

17
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:04,030
Right.

18
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:06,219
Then there's the issue of atmosphere -

19
00:01:06,219 --> 00:01:13,515
so in the first scene we needed to know how boring life was in the doctor's village in the 1950s,

20
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:18,622
so when the curtain went up on the first scene in the waiting room,

21
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:21,540
there was that long silence before anyone spoke.

22
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:27,570
And then people kept saying the same thing over and over, like 'Cold, isn't it?'

23
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:32,300
Yes, and everyone wore grey and brown, and just sat in a row.

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,120
Yes, all those details of the production.

25
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:40,531
And I have to analyse how I functioned in the group - what I found out about myself.

26
00:01:40,531 --> 00:01:44,350
I know I was so frustrated at times, when we couldn't agree.

27
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:48,730
Yes. So did one person emerge as the leader?

28
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:53,292
Sophia did. That was OK, she helped us work out exactly what to do.

29
00:01:53,292 --> 00:01:56,550
for the production. And that made me feel better, I suppose.

30
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,090
When you understood what needed doing?

31
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:04,920
Yes. And Sophia did some research, too. That was useful in developing our approach.

32
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:05,930
Like what?

33
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:11,557
Well, she found these articles from the 1950s about how relationships between children and their parents,

34
00:02:11,557 --> 00:02:16,480
or between the public and people like bank managers or the police were shifting.

35
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:21,560
Interesting. And did you have any practical problems to overcome?

36
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:25,860
Well, in the final rehearsal everything was going fine until the last scene -

37
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:29,830
that's where the doctor's first patient appears on stage on his own.

38
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:31,320
The one in the wheelchair?

39
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,100
Yes, and he had this really long speech

40
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:36,768
with the stage all dark except for one spotlight -

41
00:02:36,768 --> 00:02:41,896
and then that stuck somehow so it was shining on the wrong side of the stage...

42
00:02:41,896 --> 00:02:44,460
but anyway we got that fixed, thank goodness.

43
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:48,890
Yes, it was fine on the night.

44
00:02:48,890 --> 00:02:55,140
But while you're here, Mia, I wanted to ask you about the year abroad option

45
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:56,810
Would you recommend doing that?

46
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:02,500
Yes, definitely. It's a fantastic chance to study in another country for a year.

47
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,100
I think I'd like to do it, but it looks very competitive

48
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:08,480
there's only a limited number of places.

49
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:13,262
Yes, so next year when you are in the second year of the course,

50
00:03:13,262 --> 00:03:17,360
you need to work really hard in all your theatre studies modules.

51
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:20,340
Only students with good marks get places

52
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:24,120
you have to prove that you know your subject really well.

53
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:27,410
Right. So how did you choose where to go?

54
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:34,810
Well. I decided I wanted a programme that would fit in with what I wanted to do after I graduate,

55
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:40,360
so I looked for a university with emphasis on acting rather than directing for example.

56
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:45,095
It depends on you. Then about six months before you go,

57
00:03:45,095 --> 00:03:50,260
you have to email the scheme coordinator with your top three choices.

58
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:54,288
I had a friend who missed the deadline and didn't get her first choice,

59
00:03:54,288 --> 00:03:57,740
so you do need to get a move on at that stage.

60
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:01,280
You'll find that certain places are very popular with everyone.

61
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,690
And don't you have to write a personal statement at that stage?

62
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:05,230
Yes.

63
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:08,820
Right. I'll get some of the final year students to give me some tips

64
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:11,110
maybe see if I can read what they wrote.

65
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:15,600
I think that's a very good idea. I don't mind showing you what I did.

66
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,440
And while you're abroad don't make the mistake I made.

67
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:23,340
I got so involved I forgot all about making arrangements for

68
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,520
when I came back here for the final year.

69
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:30,740
Make sure you stay in touch so they know your choices for the optional modules.

70
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:34,410
You don't want to miss out doing your preferred specialisms.

71
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:35,270
Right.
